**JNA** (Java Native Access) is a Java library that lets Java code **call native OS libraries (C/C++)** directly without writing JNI code.

JNA Example

Calling a native C function like `strlen()`:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>net.java.dev.jna</groupId>
    <artifactId>jna</artifactId>
    <version>5.13.0</version>
</dependency>
```

```java
// Java Interface

import com.sun.jna.Library;
import com.sun.jna.Native;

public interface CLibrary extends Library {
    CLibrary INSTANCE = Native.load("c", CLibrary.class);

    int strlen(String s);
}

// Usage
public class JnaDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int length = CLibrary.INSTANCE.strlen("Hello JNA");
        System.out.println(length);
    }
}
```

🟢 No C code written
🟢 No JNI
🟢 Clean Java interface

> 💡 Since **Java 22**, the built-in **Foreign Function & Memory API** (`java.lang.foreign`) does the same without a library — the modern standard replacement for JNI and JNA.

